  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 Days...\n\nThe upper level low is slowly progressing through Central WY\ntonight with an ill-defined surface low roughly vertically\nstacked. The flow around this feature continues to advect plains\nmoisture into the region which is further aided by the gradual\nupslope. The Billings airport has already picked up a storm total\nrainfal around 3\" with rain continuing to fall. These totals are \ndue to the very saturated atmosphere through the entire column. \nEven the stratiform rainfall is producing rates up to 0.5\" per \nhour in some locations. Areas of SE MT that were under convection\nfor much of last evening were seeing 1\"/hr rates for almost 2 \nhours which produced some localized flooding.\n\nToday we will start to see the trough make its exit to the east. \nThis will be beneficial to Sheridan, WY as they will finally see \nsurface winds become more upslope. They have largely missed out on\nmost of the precipitation so far due to the persistent westerly \ncomponent of the winds resulting in continuous downslope. Across \nthe rest of the CWA, a TROWAL will set up on the backside of the\nlow which will, in part, be the reason that showers stick around \nthrough most of the day today. This feature in combination with \nthe very efficient warm conveyer belt of the low will keep much of\nthe eastern 2/3 of the CWA with scattered areas of rain through \nthe daytime hours. Due to the lack of instability, this rain will \nbe all stratiform which will keep rainfal rates, and in turn \ntotals, on the lower end compared to what we have seen already. \nThat being said, places in NE WY and SE MT could still pick up \nover another inch before all is said and done. Some CAMs are \ndepicting a banding feature of rain through the central part of \nthe CWA this evening in association with the exiting TROWAL and, \ntherefore enhanced area of CSI. This type of feature is a lot more\ncommon in wintertime systems, however is still possible in\ntransition season events such as this. \n\nIn terms of mountain snow, snow levels will remain at or above \n10,0000 ft for the remainder of the event. This could bring a \nlight accumulation to the peak of the Beartooth Pass this morning,\nin addition to mixed precip throughout the rest of event. The \nhighest peaks of the mountains (ie. Granite Peak) could pick up a \ntotal of 3-6\" of snowfall through the entire event.\n\nThe other thing to watch out for this morning is patchy fog across\nthe entire forecast area. With much of the CWA already completely\nsaturated, with rain on the gounod, fog should easily develop in \nthe light winds. Some locations will be more prone to fog \ndevelopment than others meaning that visibility could change \nsignificantly over short distances. In some areas, this saturated\nenvironment will result in low stratus clouds and minimal fog. \n\nLooking ahead to the work week, we should see much drier\nconditions. Behind the weekend low a dirty ridge builds in across\nthe area. This will allow for temperatures to slowly rebound\nthrough the week to near average. There will be a stream of\nmonsoonal moisture that is originating in Tropical Pacific\nreaching into western parts of the CWA. This could bring isolated\nshowers and storms for much of the week, but nowhere close to what\nwe are seeing this weekend. As of right now the chance for precip\nfor locations west of Billings is 30% with even lower numbers \nelsewhere. WMR",
